## C++
`Particle::Particle` (`ParticleSys.cpp:269-283`) always seeds runtime state from key 0:

```
m_alpha = m_alphaKey[0].value;
m_alphaTargetKey = 1;
computeAlphaRate();
m_color = m_colorKey[0].color;
m_colorTargetKey = 1;
computeColorRate();
```

`Alpha1`/`Color1` are authored as `low high frame` / `R: G: B: frame` (`parseRandomKeyframe` / `parseRGBColorKeyframe` at `:2683-2706`). Retail first keys use **frame 0**. Frame 0 on later keys means “unused”; on key 0 it is the start pose. Unset Color1 stays `{0,0,0}` so C++ newborns start black.

## Rust
Live `Particle::new` (`GameClient/src/effects/particle_system.rs:158-189`) defaults `alpha = 1.0`, `color = [1,1,1]`, then:

```
if particle.alpha_keys[0].frame > 0 { apply key 0 }
if particle.color_keys[0].frame > 0 { apply key 0 }
```

So the normal INI case (frame 0) is skipped. Leftover `Main/src/effects/particle_system.rs:381-383` never uses keyframes at all (linear fade by lifetime).

## Evidence
C++ ctor applies key[0] with no frame test. Live ctor’s `frame > 0` guard plus white/opaque defaults. `generate_particle_info` (`:2038-2047`) does copy sampled key 0 into `ParticleInfo`; the drop happens at `Particle::new`.

## Player-visible
Fade-in smoke/fire starts fully opaque; colored bursts (muzzle, explosion, jet exhaust) flash white until the second keyframe, then snap. Missing Color1 templates stay white instead of black.

## C++
`GameEngine::execute` (`GameEngine.cpp:768-867`) stores `prevTime` before the loop (then at end of each wait). After `update()` (which includes `TheGameClient->UPDATE()` → `W3DDisplay::draw` and its own `minTime=30` busy-wait gated on `m_useFpsLimit`), it waits:

```
DWORD limit = (1000.0f/m_maxFPS)-1;
while (TheGlobalData->m_useFpsLimit && (now - prevTime) < limit) {
    Sleep(0);
    now = timeGetTime();
}
prevTime = now;
```

`(now - prevTime)` **includes** the just-finished `update()`+draw. If the frame already took ≥ limit (or ≥ W3D 30 ms inside draw), execute does not wait extra. Effective period is max(work, limit), not work+limit.

`getTimeMultiplier()<=1 && !isTimeFast()` gates the whole throttle; TiVO+replay skips it.

## Rust
Live windowed `AboutToWait` (`run_loop.rs:810-817`) always:

```
drive_frame(...); // update + render (W3D analog already inside)
next_redraw_at = Instant::now() + engine.live_present_interval().unwrap_or(ZERO);
```

`live_present_interval` (`run_loop.rs:927-947`) returns `max(exec_ms, draw_ms)` ≈ 29 ms when `use_fps_limit` (exec `(1000/maxFPS)-1`, draw `30-1`). That duration is added **after** `drive_frame` returns.

hq-nzr3 closed the 60→45 constant. This remaining gap is the wait *placement*: C++ period includes work; live WaitUntil is extra idle after work.

## Evidence
```
// GameEngine.cpp:856-867 — wait measured from prevTime (previous wait end / this iteration start)
DWORD now = timeGetTime();
DWORD limit = (1000.0f/m_maxFPS)-1;
while (TheGlobalData->m_useFpsLimit && (now - prevTime) < limit) { Sleep(0); now = timeGetTime(); }
prevTime = now;
```

```
// run_loop.rs:814-817
drive_frame(engine, current_window, &mut runtime_host_bridge, true);
current_window.request_redraw();
next_redraw_at = Instant::now()
    + engine.live_present_interval().unwrap_or(Duration::ZERO);
```

## Player-visible effect
A 40 ms update+render frame is ~40 ms in C++ (limit already satisfied) but ~40+29 ms (~14–17 FPS) on the live host. Slow frames (shell map, model load) feel half-speed versus retail. Unrelated to the already-closed 45 vs 60 cap constant.

## C++
`GameEngine::init` (`GameEngine.cpp:360-361`) constructs `TheGameLODManager` after `parseCommandLine` and calls `TheGameLODManager->init()` before Water/Weather INI.

`GameLODManager::init` (`GameLOD.cpp:244-339`) loads `Data\INI\GameLOD.ini` + `GameLODPresets.ini`, snapshots Custom from GlobalData, reads `OptionPreferences` (`Options.ini`), and `setStaticLODLevel(userSetDetail)`. Custom detail copies TextureReduction, 3D/2D shadows, building occlusion, particle cap, dynamic LOD, **UseFPSLimit**, lightmap/cloud/water/heat, extra animations, trees.

`GameEngine.cpp:535` then `setFramesPerSecondLimit(TheGlobalData->m_framesPerSecondLimit)` so the execute cap matches the options-applied GlobalData.

## Rust
Parity helper `load_game_lod_ini_presets_and_options` exists (`Common/src/common/game_lod.rs:489-560`) but is only called from unused crate `GameEngine::init` (`Common/src/common/game_engine.rs:417`).

Live boot is `CnCGameEngine::new` (`Main/src/cnc_game_engine/boot.rs`). Grep of `GeneralsRust/Code/Main` finds **zero** `load_game_lod_ini_presets_and_options` / `GameLOD.ini` / `init_game_lod_manager` calls.

Live `apply_live_draw_dynamic_lod` (`run_loop.rs:951-954`) reads `get_game_lod_manager()`, which lazy-inits an empty default manager (no INI, no Options.ini, no `setStaticLODLevel`).

## Evidence
```
// GameEngine.cpp:360-361
TheGameLODManager = MSGNEW("GameEngineSubsystem") GameLODManager;
TheGameLODManager->init();
```

```
// game_lod.rs:489-491
/// C++ `GameLODManager::init`: load GameLOD.ini, GameLODPresets.ini, snapshot
/// Custom from GlobalData, apply OptionPreferences, then setStaticLODLevel.
pub fn load_game_lod_ini_presets_and_options() {
```

No call from live `boot.rs` / `run_loop.rs` / `shell.rs`.

## Player-visible effect
Options.ini detail (High/Medium/Low/Custom), FPS-limit toggle, tree/shadow/lightmap/cloud/water/heat flags, and static LOD particle/animation presets never apply on the live host. Dynamic LOD during `W3DDisplay::draw` analog uses empty default thresholds, so the 45 Hz execute cap / visual quality do not follow retail options.

## C++
`GameEngine::update` (`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/Common/GameEngine.cpp:732`) always calls `TheRadar->UPDATE()` before audio/client/logic.

`Radar::update` (`Radar.cpp:275-304`) each logic frame:
- expires `m_event[i]` when `active && createFrame && thisFrame > dieFrame`
- if `m_queueTerrainRefreshFrame` is set and `getFrame() - queue > RADAR_QUEUE_TERRAIN_REFRESH_DELAY`, calls `refreshTerrain(TheTerrainLogic)`

Attack/infiltrate/upgrade pings therefore disappear after `dieFrame`, and deferred radar terrain rebuilds (bridges, water, script refresh) actually run.

## Rust
The live host `drive_frame` (`Main/src/cnc_game_engine/run_loop.rs:206-226`) never calls Common `RadarSystem::update`.

`update_internal` ticks `update_runtime_subsystems`, whose Radar slot is `RadarSubsystem::update` (`Main/src/subsystem_manager.rs:690-692`) — a documented no-op ("handled by gamelogic radar notifier"). Main has zero `radar.update(` call sites.

The C++-parity implementation exists (`Common/src/common/system/radar/mod.rs:1080-1119`) and is only invoked from unused crate `GameEngine::update_frame` (`Common/src/common/game_engine.rs:648-650`), not the live run loop.

Distinct from already-filed `addObject` color / `newMap` hook / `tryEvent` distance (hq-jdc6m, hq-xii2r, hq-yfai9).

## Evidence
```
// GameEngine.cpp:732
TheRadar->UPDATE();

// Radar.cpp:288-300
if (m_event[i].active && m_event[i].createFrame && thisFrame > m_event[i].dieFrame)
    m_event[i].active = FALSE;
if (m_queueTerrainRefreshFrame != 0 &&
    TheGameLogic->getFrame() - m_queueTerrainRefreshFrame > RADAR_QUEUE_TERRAIN_REFRESH_DELAY)
    refreshTerrain(TheTerrainLogic);
```

```
// subsystem_manager.rs:690-692
fn update(&mut self, _dt: f32) -> Result<()> {
    // Radar updates are handled by gamelogic radar notifier
    Ok(())
}
```

## Player-visible effect
Radar event markers (under-attack, infiltration, stealth, upgrade) never time out on the live HUD. Queued terrain refreshes after bridge/water/script changes never rebuild the radar map after the C++ delay.

## C++
`PhysicsBehavior::update` (PhysicsUpdate.cpp:649-663): if OBJECT_STATUS_BRAKING, projectiles get *no* position integration (\"Projectiles never stop braking once they start\" + locomotor 3D cheat owns the pose). Non-projectiles only integrate Z.

## Rust
`update_simple` (`physics_update.rs:764-768`):
```
if obj.test_status(Braking) && !obj.is_kind_of(Projectile) {
    pos.z += state.vel.z;
} else {
    pos += state.vel;
}
```
Braking projectiles take the else branch and full-move.

## Evidence
C++: `if (BRAKING) { if (!PROJECTILE) Adjust_Z; }` — projectile body empty.
Rust condition is `braking && !projectile` for Z-only; projectile+braking → `pos += vel`.

## Player-visible effect
Missiles/shells that entered the braking cheat keep sliding on physics velocity instead of the locomotor snap-to-target, overshooting or weaving off the aim point.

## Split hint
Match the C++ nested if; do not invert the projectile test.

## C++
`PlaceEventTranslator::translateGameMessage` (`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/GameClient/MessageStream/PlaceEventTranslator.cpp:269-284`). On `isLocationLegalToBuild != LBC_OK` it: displays `displayCantBuildMessage(lbc)`, plays the builder template `VoiceNoBuild` per-unit sound, plays UI `NoCanDoSound`, then unhooks the placement anchor with `setPlacementStart(NULL)` so the player can retry.

## Rust
Live host `place_structure_from_ui` (`GeneralsRust/Code/Main/src/cnc_game_engine/ui_commands.rs:1268-1290`) only pushes `lbc_help_message_residual` text and re-arms placement. Crate `PlaceEventTranslator::handle_place_click` (`GeneralsRust/Code/GameEngine/GameClient/src/message_stream/place_event_translator.rs:241-247`) calls `display_cant_build_message` and `set_placement_start(None)` only. Neither path plays `VoiceNoBuild` or `NoCanDoSound`.

## Evidence
C++:
```
TheInGameUI->displayCantBuildMessage( lbc );
AudioEventRTS sound = *builderObj->getTemplate()->getPerUnitSound( "VoiceNoBuild" );
TheAudio->addAudioEvent( &sound );
static AudioEventRTS noCanDoSound( "NoCanDoSound" );
TheAudio->addAudioEvent( &noCanDoSound );
```
Rust host: `let msg = lbc_help_message_residual(lbc); ... push_info_message(msg);` — no audio.

## Player-visible effect
Clicking an illegal build spot shows a text reason but the dozer never says "can't build" and the UI beep is silent.

## C++
`PlaceEventTranslator::translateGameMessage` (`PlaceEventTranslator.cpp:166-197`) re-checks `TheBuildAssistant->canMakeUnit(builderObj, build)` on click-up. `CANMAKE_NO_MONEY` plays `TheEva->setShouldPlay(EVA_InsufficientFunds)` plus `GUI:NotEnoughMoneyToBuild` and keeps placement armed. Queue-full / parking-full / maxed-out show the matching GUI string. Other failures cancel placement via `placeBuildAvailable(NULL,NULL)`.

## Rust
Live host `place_structure_from_ui` (`ui_commands.rs:1266-1300`) only tests `host_legal_build_code_at_for_builder` (terrain/overlap). It never calls `BuildAssistant::can_make_unit` and never plays `EvaMessage::InsufficientFunds`. Crate `handle_place_click` (`place_event_translator.rs:221-236`) reimplements a local `can_make_unit` and shows the GUI string, but still never sets EVA.

## Evidence
C++:
```
if (cmt == CANMAKE_NO_MONEY) {
  TheEva->setShouldPlay(EVA_InsufficientFunds);
  TheInGameUI->message( "GUI:NotEnoughMoneyToBuild" );
  break;
}
```
Host illegal path is LBC-only; no `can_make_unit` / `InsufficientFunds` at confirm.

## Player-visible effect
A building armed while the player still has cash can be confirmed after a hacker steals the money: no EVA "insufficient funds", no queue/maxed toast, and the order may still be issued. Distinct from closed hq-egcq (BuildAssistant table) — this is the PlaceEvent confirm call site.

## C++
`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/Common/Thing/ThingTemplate.cpp:220` field table: `{ "Locomotor", AIUpdateModuleData::parseLocomotorSet, NULL, 0 }`.
`AIUpdate.cpp:141-175` `parseLocomotorSet` casts the instance to `ThingTemplate*`, requires `friend_getAIModuleInfo()` (the already-parsed AIUpdate module data), `scanIndexList` against `TheLocomotorSetNames` (`SET_NORMAL`, `SET_NORMAL_UPGRADED`, `SET_FREEFALL`, `SET_WANDER`, `SET_PANIC`, …), looks up each name in `TheLocomotorStore`, and `push_back`s the `LocomotorTemplate*` into `AIUpdateModuleData::m_locomotorTemplates[set]`. Multiple `Locomotor = SET_* Name` lines accumulate. `AIUpdateModuleData::buildFieldParse` (`AIUpdate.cpp:106-124`) does **not** include Locomotor — retail Object.ini puts it as a sibling of `Behavior`, not inside the module body. Re-specifying a set throws unless `INI_LOAD_CREATE_OVERRIDES`.

## Rust
`thing_template.rs` `parse_locomotor_field` (2568-2579) splits the value and inserts `HashMap<String, Vec<AsciiString>>` on the template only. Nothing implements `friend_getAIModuleInfo`. GameLogic `AIUpdateModuleData.locomotor_sets` (`ai_update_interface.rs`) is populated only from a Locomotor field *inside* an AIUpdate module body (Jet/Dozer/etc. parsers). `ThingTemplate::locomotor_sets()` / `locomotor_set_names()` have no GameLogic callers. `consume_ini_properties` (`thing_factory.rs:323-332`) treats Locomotor as a non-repeatable key, so a second `Locomotor = SET_NORMAL_UPGRADED …` line overwrites the first.

Closed `hq-aoq2` only stored the names on the template; the C++ side-effect (write into AI module data) is still missing.

## Evidence
```
// C++ AIUpdate.cpp:143-174
ThingTemplate *tt = (ThingTemplate *)instance;
AIUpdateModuleData *self = tt->friend_getAIModuleInfo();
if (!self) { throw INI_INVALID_DATA; }
LocomotorSetType set = (LocomotorSetType)INI::scanIndexList(... TheLocomotorSetNames);
self->m_locomotorTemplates[set].push_back(lt);
```
```
// Rust thing_template.rs:2578
self.locomotor_sets.insert(set_name.to_string(), names);
```

## Player-visible effect
Retail units list `Locomotor = SET_NORMAL CrusaderLocomotor` (and often SET_NORMAL_UPGRADED / WANDER / PANIC) at Object scope. AIUpdate spawns with an empty locomotor map, so tanks/infantry/aircraft never pick a loco set — they do not walk, drive, or fly at authored speeds, and upgraded locos never apply.

## Split hint
Keep parse on ThingTemplate; add `friend_get_ai_module_info()` and write into the live AIUpdateModuleData (or copy `locomotor_sets` at module instantiate). Make `Locomotor` a repeatable consume_ini key.

## C++
`LocomotorStore::parseLocomotorTemplateDefinition` (Locomotor.cpp:583-614) loads every `Locomotor <Name>` block from Locomotor.ini into `TheLocomotorStore`. `AIUpdate` / `LocomotorSet::addLocomotor` instantiate by that retail name (`BasicHumanLocomotor`, `HumveeLocomotor`, `ComancheLocomotor`, …).

## Rust
Live AI path (`ai_update_interface.rs`, `ai_loco.rs`) looks up `crate::locomotor::LOCOMOTOR_STORE`. That store (`locomotor/core_impl/set_store.rs:358-371`) only `register_template`s seven hardcoded stubs (`Infantry`, `Wheeled`, `Tracked`, `Hover`, `Thrust`, `Wings`, `Climber`). Retail INI names never appear. Common `ini_locomotor` store is populated from archives / `locomotor_bootstrap.rs` but is not copied into the GameLogic store.

## Evidence
```
pub static LOCOMOTOR_STORE: Lazy<Arc<LocomotorStore>> = Lazy::new(|| {
    store.register_template(LocomotorTemplate::new_infantry("Infantry".to_string()));
    // ... Wheeled/Tracked/Hover/Thrust/Wings/Climber only
});
```
`LOCOMOTOR_STORE.create_locomotor(template_name)` with `BasicHumanLocomotor` returns None.

## Player-visible effect
Units spawn without their authored locomotor (or fall back to a generic stub). Wander/climb/hover/thrust speeds, surfaces, and SET_PANIC/SET_TAXIING members never apply.

## Split hint
`locomotor/ini_bridge.rs` (copy Common store → GameLogic store), drop hardcoded stub registration.

## C++
`SelectionTranslator` `MSG_META_SELECT_TEAM0-9` in `SelectionXlat.cpp` 1085-1103: if the same group is selected again within 20 logic frames, `TheTacticalView->lookAt` centers on the last live object in the hotkey squad.

## Rust
`GameClient/src/message_stream/selection_xlat.rs` `handle_select_control_group`: detects double-tap (`time_since_last.as_millis() < DOUBLE_CLICK_TIME_MS`) then only logs. Comment: "Would jump camera to group location here". No `look_at` / tactical view call.

## Evidence
```
if time_since_last.as_millis() < DOUBLE_CLICK_TIME_MS as u128 && group as i32 == self.last_group_sel_group {
    debug!("Double-tap select control group {}", group);
