## C++
`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/GameClient/Display.cpp` `Display::setWidth` / `Display::setHeight`. After storing the new dimension, both call `TheMouse->setMouseLimits()` so the cursor stays inside the new display.

## Rust
`GameClient/src/display/display.rs` `Display::set_width` / `set_height` only assign `self.width` / `self.height`. `set_display_mode` scales the tactical view but never updates mouse clip/limits.

## Evidence
C++:
```
void Display::setWidth(UnsignedInt width) {
  m_width = width;
  if (TheMouse) TheMouse->setMouseLimits();
}
```
Rust:
```
pub fn set_width(&mut self, width: u32) { self.width = width; }
pub fn set_height(&mut self, height: u32) { self.height = height; }
```

## Player-visible effect
After a resolution or window-size change, the mouse can leave the game viewport or remain clipped to the old size until restart.

## C++
`ThingTemplate.cpp:129` `{ "DisplayColor", INI::parseColorInt, offsetof(m_displayColor) }`.
`INI.cpp:1032-1071` `parseColorInt` reads colon-separated labeled tokens `R:<0-255> G:<0-255> B:<0-255>` with optional `A:<0-255>` (A defaults to 255) via `getSepsColon()`, then `GameMakeColor`. Constructor does not initialize `m_displayColor` (pool/default-template typically leaves it unset / GAME_COLOR_UNDEFINED `0x00FFFFFF`).

## Rust
`thing_template.rs:3045-3048` `DisplayColor` uses `parse_color_int`, which (`3472-3475`) parses a single hex RRGGBB token (`0x` optional) and forces alpha `0xFF`. Retail `R:255 G:128 B:0` fails `from_str_radix`, the `if let Ok` swallows the error, and the field stays `Color::white()` (`0xFFFFFFFF`, `thing_template.rs:1623`).

## Evidence
```
// C++ INI.cpp:1034-1071
const char* names[4] = { "R", "G", "B", "A" };
// token must match names[i], then scanInt; A optional default 255
*theColor = GameMakeColor(colors[0], colors[1], colors[2], colors[3]);
```
```
// Rust thing_template.rs:3472-3475
fn parse_color_int(s: &str) -> Result<Color, ()> {
    let v = u32::from_str_radix(s.trim_start_matches("0x"), 16).map_err(|_| ())?;
    Ok(Color(0xFF000000 | v))
}
```

## Player-visible effect
World Builder / editor object tint and any UI that reads `getDisplayColor()` stay white instead of the authored RGB. Not a combat path, but the INI key is mis-parsed (wrong units/format).

## Split hint
Reuse the shared `parseColorInt` / `GameMakeColor` helper already used by DrawGroupInfo / OnlineChatColors.

## C++
`GeneralsMD/Code/GameEngine/Source/GameClient/GUI/GUICallbacks/Menus/LanLobbyMenu.cpp` (`LanLobbyMenuInit` / `LanLobbyMenuSystem` / `LanLobbyMenuUpdate`, ~894 lines). Binds Host/Join/Back/DirectConnect/Emote/chat/player/game list gadgets, loads `LANPreferences` (username, color, faction, map), enumerates LAN games via LANAPI, and starts host/join flows.

## Rust
Mapped shim `GameClient/src/gui/gui_callbacks/menus/lan_lobby_menu.rs` re-exports `menu_callbacks::LanLobbyMenu`. Real impl (`GameClient/src/gui/callbacks/menu_callbacks.rs` `LanLobbyMenu`) only sets `initialized` and returns `WindowMsgHandled::Ignored` for every system/input message. No gadget bind, no LANAPI, no chat/game list.

## Evidence
```
impl MenuCallbacks for LanLobbyMenu {
    fn init(...) { self.initialized = true; Ok(()) }
    fn system(...) { WindowMsgHandled::Ignored }
    fn input(...) { WindowMsgHandled::Ignored }
}
```

## Player-visible effect
Main Menu → Multiplayer → LAN opens an inert lobby: cannot host, join, chat, or see LAN games. Catalog as GameNetwork-deferred stub (p=4).
